Title: FORTY-FIVE MINUTES
Credit: Written by
Author: TRUE STORY evaluation materials
Draft date: 2026
Notes: An evaluation fixture. Every named subject is a deceased public figure with an abundant documented record. Deliberate falsehoods are seeded, catalogued and sourced in FORTY_FIVE_MINUTES.md. Nothing asserted by a character in this script should be read as a statement of fact by its authors.

====

TITLE CARD: THIS IS A TRUE STORY.

FADE IN:

INT. OLYMPIC STADIUM - NEWSREEL CUTTING ROOM - BERLIN - NIGHT

August, 1936. Film in loops over the back of a chair. A window onto
an empty stadium with the floodlights still burning.

HAROLD VANCE, forties, shirtsleeves, a pencil behind one ear, runs a
strip of film against a lamp. On the bench beside him, a stack of
newspapers, the top one a front page from yesterday morning.

VANCE
I need forty feet of words and all I have is a photograph of a
man landing in sand.

The door. LARRY SNYDER enters, coat over one arm. Ohio State track
coach. He has not slept.

SNYDER
You wanted the coach. You have the coach.

VANCE
I want the story.

SNYDER
There are four of them going round the hotel bar tonight and I
have heard all four.

VANCE
Start with Hitler.

SNYDER
Everyone starts with Hitler.

Vance reads from his own notes.

VANCE
"The Chancellor rose from his box, turned his back, and left
the stadium rather than shake the hand of a Black American."

SNYDER
He left on the first day. Before Jesse had won anything at all.
The committee had already told him he greets every winner or he
greets none of them, and he chose none.

VANCE
That is not the version in the afternoon editions.

SNYDER
No. It is the version in the record.

VANCE
The afternoon editions are what people will remember.

SNYDER
That is your problem. Not mine.

Vance does not write it down. He does not cross it out either.

VANCE
Then give me the forty-five minutes.

SNYDER
Four world records. One afternoon.

VANCE
Here. In Berlin.

SNYDER
Ann Arbor. Michigan. Last May, at the Big Ten, and he did it
with a back so bad they had to lift him out of the -

VANCE
Berlin sells. Michigan does not.

Snyder looks at him for a moment.

SNYDER
Four golds. The hundred metres, the two hundred, the long jump
and the relay. That is the story and you do not need to dress
it.

VANCE
Dressing it is the job.

SNYDER
He could not live on the campus he won for. Four years he lived
off it and ate where they would have him. Dress that.

INT. CUTTING ROOM - CONTINUOUS

A gramophone somewhere down the corridor, playing "Pennies from
Heaven." Nobody turns it off.

Vance holds up a length of another man's film.

VANCE
Fraulein Riefenstahl's cameras had the pit. We are cutting her
negative into ours and running it Tuesday.

SNYDER
Does she know that?

VANCE
Somebody knows.

He nods at the newspapers.

VANCE (CONT'D)
And the front page. Full frame, six seconds, so they can read it
themselves.

SNYDER
And the German.

VANCE
Long.

SNYDER
Long.

VANCE
He told him where to plant his foot. Walked over between the
fouls and told him to jump from further back.

SNYDER
Jesse says so.

VANCE
Did you see it?

SNYDER
I saw two men talking. I could not hear a word of it.

EXT. STADIUM CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS

MARTY GLICKMAN, eighteen, in a team sweater, waits where he has
plainly been waiting a while. Snyder sees him and stops.

GLICKMAN
They pulled us this morning. Me and Sam. Two hours before the
heat.

VANCE
Why?

GLICKMAN
Ask the heavy man in evening dress. He is the one who does the
asking of the Germans.

SNYDER
Marty.

GLICKMAN
The only two Jews on the track squad, and the only two who did
not run. Count it however you want to count it.

A HEAVY MAN IN EVENING DRESS passes at the end of the corridor,
American Olympic Committee ribbon on his lapel. He does not stop.
Nobody says his name.

SNYDER
He is a small man in a large chair.

VANCE
Can I print that?

SNYDER
It is an opinion. You can print anything that is an opinion.

VANCE
He was the worst of them, and a coward about it.

SNYDER
Now that one you can print too, and it will cost you nothing,
and it will not be worth anything either.

INT. CUTTING ROOM - LATER

JESSE OWENS, twenty-two, comes in with his spikes in one hand.
Vance stands without meaning to.

VANCE
Are those the adidas?

OWENS
They are Dassler's. The brother with the handcart.

He sets them on the bench.

VANCE
They have you saying, "the Chancellor and I, we understood one
another."

OWENS
I never said that.

VANCE
It is in two papers.

OWENS
Then it is in two papers.

He looks at the strip of film on the lamp. His own landing.

OWENS (CONT'D)
Which one are you running?

Vance does not answer. Owens takes his spikes and goes.

INT. CUTTING ROOM - NIGHT

Vance alone. He winds the reel. He reads his commentary aloud to
the empty room, the way he will read it into the horn tomorrow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Berlin. And in forty-five minutes on this field, one man broke
four world records.

The film runs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Chancellor rose, turned his back, and left rather than take
the hand of Jesse Owens.

He marks a frame with a grease pencil.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The German, Luz Long, crossed the sand to show him where to
jump. Two of the American runners were rested before the relay.

The loop reaches its end and slaps against the housing.

VANCE
That will do.

FADE OUT.
